{{smaller block|The Jewish nation was an ''anathema'' destined to destruction. St. Paul . . . says he could wish to save them from it, and to become an ''anathema,'' and to be destroyed himself.
{{right|''Locke'', Paraphrase of Rom. ix. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>It is God’s will, the Holy Father’s will,
And Philip’s will, and mine, that he should burn.
He is pronounced ''anathema.''</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Queen Mary, iv. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' A curse or denunciation pronounced with religious solemnity by ecclesiastical authority, involving excommunication. {{smaller|This species of excommunication was practised in the ancient churches against incorrigible offenders. Churches were warned not to receive them, magistrates and private persons were admonished not to harbor or maintain them, and priests were enjoined not to converse with them or attend their funerals. Also called ''judiciary anathema.'' The formula, “which if anybody deny let him be ''anathema,''” is commonly added to the decrees of ecclesiastical councils, and especially to the doctrinal canons of ecumenical councils. It is denied by some theologians that the idea of a curse properly belongs to the anathema as used in the Christian church. See ''excommunication.''}}
{{smaller block|In pronouncing ''anathema'' against wilful heretics, the Church does but declare that they are excluded from her communion, and that they must, if they continue obstinate, perish eternally.
{{right|''Cath. Dict.''}}}}
Hence—'''3.''' Any imprecation of divine punishment; a curse; an execration.
{{smaller block|She fled to London, followed by the ''anathemas'' of both.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Vanity Fair.}}}}
{{smaller block|Drawing his falchion and uttering a thousand ''anathe-''}}

