'''anathematization''' (a-nath″ē-mat-i-zā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ML. ''anathematizatio''(''n''-), < LL. ''anathematizare'', pp. *''anathematizatus'', anathematize: see ''anathematize''.] The act of anathematizing or denouncing as accursed; excommunication. Also spelled ''anathematisation.''
{{smaller block|Prohibiting the . . . ''anathematization'' of persons deceased in the peace of the church.
{{right|''Barrow'', The Pope’s Supremacy.}}}}

'''anathematize''' (a-nath′ē-mạ-tīz), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''anathematized'', ppr. ''anathematizing''. [= F. ''anathématiser'', < LL. ''anathematizare'', < Gr. ἀναθεματίζειν, devote to evil, excommunicate, curse, < ἀνάθεμα: see ''anathema''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To pronounce an anathema against; denounce; curse.
{{smaller block|The priests continued to exorcise the possessed, to prosecute witches, and to ''anathematise'' as infidels all who questioned the crime. {{right|''Lecky'', Rationalism, I. 115.}}
At length his words found vent, and for three days he [William the Testy] kept up a constant discharge, ''anathematizing'' the Yankees, man, woman, and child.
{{right|''Irving'', Knickerbocker, p. 222.}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To pronounce anathemas; curse.
{{smaller block|Well may mankind shriek, inarticulately ''anathematizing'' as they can. {{right|''Carlyle'', French Rev., III. i. 6.}}}}
Also spelled ''anathematise.''

